WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.697
In superhero movies, the bad guy always loses,
right?

00:00:03.697 --> 00:00:07.359
Well, "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" flips
the script on that a bit.

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:10.125
Here's how Namor comes away with a victory
of sorts.

00:00:10.125 --> 00:00:11.926
Warning: Spoilers ahead.

00:00:11.926 --> 00:00:15.889
Namor has a significantly different origin
story in "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever"

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:17.275
than he does in the comics.

00:00:17.275 --> 00:00:22.199
One of his many MCU names is "K'uk'ulkan,"
which is also the name of a feathered serpent

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:24.000
god in Mesoamerican mythology.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.778
"They call him K'uk'ulkan, the feather serpent
god."

00:00:29.778 --> 00:00:34.480
Instead of ruling Atlantis, he rules over
Talokan, a hybrid of Atlantis from the comics

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:40.228
and Aztec myth's Tlalocan, the level of the
afterlife reserved for those who died water-related deaths.

00:00:40.228 --> 00:00:44.550
With these changes, Marvel could avoid confusion
with the DC Universe's Atlantis and had a

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:49.010
chance to create a rich backstory for yet
another culture that, like Wakanda, was founded

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.651
by people of color and flourished into a highly
advanced society.

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:56.800
In an interview with the Los Angeles Times,
actor Tenoch Huerta talked about what it meant

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.900
to him to be playing a character in the Marvel
Cinematic Universe that has darker skin.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:01.601
He said,

00:01:14.418 --> 00:01:19.068
In the comics, Namor is the son of an English
explorer and Princess Fen of Atlantis.

00:01:19.068 --> 00:01:22.333
He has none of the Mesoamerican origins that
he has in the movie.

00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:26.650
But one element from the comics that persists
is Namor's shifting allegiances depending

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.220
on what best suits his ends.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:33.057
At the end of "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever,"
Shuri defeats Namor, but takes mercy on him,

00:01:33.057 --> 00:01:34.354
offering to let him yield.

00:01:34.354 --> 00:01:38.790
He does so, and the two fly back to their
warriors together, demonstrating the truce.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:43.510
Later, Namor reveals to Namora that he believes
the surface world will come for Wakanda to

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:48.121
take their vibranium, and when that happens,
Wakanda will look to Talokan as an ally.

00:01:48.121 --> 00:01:52.379
"I need to know if Wakanda is an ally or an
enemy."

00:01:52.379 --> 00:01:55.220
This is basically what Namor wanted from the
beginning.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.969
The king originally went to war with Wakanda
because they refused to turn over Riri Williams,

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:04.600
the young scientist who built the vibranium-detecting
apparatus, nor would they agree to go to war

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.474
with him against the surface world.

00:02:06.474 --> 00:02:10.660
Yet this truce signifies that they may be
willing to align with Talokan in the future,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.857
should they ever find themselves under attack.

00:02:12.857 --> 00:02:16.860
Time Magazine has stated that Namor will be
returning, though they seem to be basing this

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.860
more on the position that Namor finds himself
in at the end of the movie rather than any

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.663
official announcement from Marvel.

00:02:22.663 --> 00:02:26.201
It definitely does seem like we'll be seeing
more of the character, and that he'll be as

00:02:26.201 --> 00:02:28.390
opportunistic as his comics counterpart.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:33.739
Namor made his comics debut in 1939 in "Motion
Picture Funnies Weekly" #1.

00:02:33.739 --> 00:02:37.758
In his first appearance, Namor viciously attacks
the crew of a salvage vessel trying to bring

00:02:37.758 --> 00:02:39.058
up lost treasure.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.456
This is followed by Namor's mother telling
him the story of why they don't trust surface-dwellers:

00:02:43.456 --> 00:02:46.227
Some of them attacked Atlantis and killed
many of their people.

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:51.627
So even in his brutal first appearance, Namor's
actions can be justified as defending his nation.

00:02:51.627 --> 00:02:55.886
Namor often switches sides between heroes
and villains and never really settles down

00:02:55.886 --> 00:02:56.910
as one or the other.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:02.927
In 1964's "X-Men" #6, Magneto tries to get
Namor to join his Brotherhood of Evil Mutants,

00:03:02.927 --> 00:03:07.105
but Namor quickly becomes disenchanted with
Magneto's leadership and turns on him.

00:03:07.105 --> 00:03:10.075
In "Dark Avengers" #8, Namor joins the X-Men.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.730
But even in his time as a member of a superhero
team, Namor is morally ambiguous.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:20.320
In "Avengers vs. X-Men" #8, Namor floods Wakanda
during a conflict with the Avengers, in defiance

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.380
of direct orders from Cyclops.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.961
That scene was clearly an inspiration for
one of Namor's more malicious acts in "Wakanda Forever."

00:03:26.961 --> 00:03:30.880
It's hard to call the MCU's version of Namor
good or evil, either.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.680
He does some extreme things, but in the name
of protecting his nation from a legitimate

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:36.766
threat from the people of the surface.

00:03:36.766 --> 00:03:41.599
His actions put him on a similar level of
moral ambiguity to some other MCU villains

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:46.435
like Ghost or Killmonger; they all have justified
ends from their perspective, but do immoral

00:03:46.435 --> 00:03:47.700
things to achieve them.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.909
With the movie leaving the door open for Namor
to return, it's possible he'll return as an

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:54.530
antihero rather than a full-on antagonist.

